## Day 2 — First Spring Boot Microservice

Day 2 was about taking the environment we prepared on Day 1 and building the **first working backend service** for our Enterprise Financial Data Platform.

The main goal was simple:

> **Create `customer-service`, run it successfully, expose an API, and verify that the service is healthy.**

### 1. Created the backend structure

We started with a clean project structure:

```text
enterprise-financial-data-platform/
└── backend/
    └── customer-service/
```

`customer-service` is our first microservice. Eventually it will handle customer creation, updates, retrieval, validation, database operations, and customer-related events.

### 2. Created the Spring Boot project

We generated the service using Spring Initializr with:

```text
Build Tool     : Maven
Language       : Java
Java           : 21
Packaging      : JAR

Group          : com.financialplatform
Artifact       : customer-service
Package        : com.financialplatform.customer
```

We selected four initial dependencies:

**Spring Web** — gives us Spring MVC, REST controller support, JSON handling, and embedded Tomcat.

**Spring Boot Actuator** — provides operational endpoints such as application health.

**Validation** — we'll use this soon to validate incoming customer requests.

**Lombok** — will reduce repetitive Java code such as getters, setters, constructors, and builders.

We intentionally **didn't add Oracle, Kafka, Redis, Security, AWS, etc. yet**. We'll introduce each technology when the project actually needs it.

### 3. Started the application

Our main class is:

```text
CustomerServiceApplication
```

When we ran it, Spring Boot started its embedded Tomcat server on:

```text
localhost:8080
```

The important console messages were essentially:

```text
Tomcat started on port 8080
Started CustomerServiceApplication
```

This proved that Java, Maven, Spring Boot, our dependencies, and the embedded web server were all configured correctly.

### 4. Added application configuration

We decided to use:

```text
application.properties
```

instead of YAML.

We configured the service name, port `8080`, and Actuator endpoint exposure.

This file will grow later when we introduce database connections, Kafka, security, logging, environment profiles, and other configurations.

### 5. Tested Spring Boot Actuator

We tested:

```text
GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/health
```

and received:

```text
200 OK
```

with the application reporting an `UP` status.

This is more important than it initially looks.

In production, systems such as **Kubernetes and monitoring platforms** need a reliable way to determine whether an application instance is healthy.

Later we'll build on this with:

```text
Liveness
Readiness
Metrics
Prometheus
Grafana
CloudWatch
```

So Actuator is our first small step toward **production observability**.

### 6. Created our first REST Controller

We created:

```text
com.financialplatform.customer
└── controller
    └── CustomerController
```

and exposed:

```text
GET /api/v1/customers/health
```

We deliberately used:

```text
/api/v1/
```

because we're establishing an **API versioning convention** from the beginning.

### 7. Tested our API using Postman

We called:

```text
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/customers/health
```

and received:

```text
200 OK
```

with a JSON response confirming that `customer-service` was running.

That verified our first complete HTTP flow:

```text
Postman
   ↓
HTTP Request
   ↓
Tomcat :8080
   ↓
Spring DispatcherServlet
   ↓
CustomerController
   ↓
Java Method
   ↓
ResponseEntity
   ↓
JSON Response
   ↓
Postman
```

### 8. Understood the important annotations

We introduced several fundamental Spring concepts.

**`@RestController`** tells Spring that the class handles REST API requests and returns response data.

**`@RequestMapping`** defines the base URL for the controller.

**`@GetMapping`** maps an HTTP GET request to a Java method.

**`ResponseEntity`** allows us to control the HTTP response, including status code and response body.

These concepts will appear throughout almost every Spring Boot REST API we build.

### 9. Followed a Git feature workflow

Instead of developing directly in `main`, we used:

```text
main
 ↑
develop
 ↑
feature/customer-service-setup
```

Our feature work was committed and pushed to GitHub.

This is important because we're not treating GitHub simply as a place to store code. We're practicing a development workflow closer to what we'd use on a software team.

